WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:10.520
At conception, a sperm, carrying either&nbsp;
an X or Y chromosome, fuses with an egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:17.640
which carries an X chromosome. In this process,&nbsp;
a single cell, called a zygote, is formed. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:25.160
combination of chromosomes inside the cell&nbsp;
decides if the baby will be a boy or a girl.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:32.560
An X and a Y chromosome create a male.&nbsp;
And two X a female. Around 1 in 5000 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.280
us happen to get a different chromosome&nbsp;
combination. They are sometimes referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:44.600
to as intersex and are proof that something as&nbsp;
binary as male and female can be on a spectrum.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.720
For simplicity, we will&nbsp;
compare typical men and women.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:57.080
At Birth, children are typically identified&nbsp;
as boys or girls. Boys tend to be slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:03.400
larger and heavier than girls. Their&nbsp;
heads are often a bit bigger too!

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.800
Within weeks, cultural norms start to reinforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.480
traditional gender roles. This makes&nbsp;
it difficult for scientists to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:18.320
what behavioral differences are biological&nbsp;
and which ones are socially constructed.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.160
During Childhood, boys tend to be more physically&nbsp;
active, exhibiting greater interest in objects and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:32.560
exploration. They also begin to have a better&nbsp;
understanding of three-dimensional space.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.400
Girls are less active, take a higher&nbsp;
interest in people, and seem to form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.360
a better understanding of them. They also&nbsp;
learn new languages faster. At just 16 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:52.560
girls, on average, have a vocabulary of&nbsp;
95 words, more than 3 times that of boys.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.320
During Puberty, kids become&nbsp;
teenagers. Their bodies change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:02.920
and the differences between boys&nbsp;
and girls become more obvious.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:09.640
Around the ages of 8 to 13, girls start to&nbsp;
get taller, their body shape begins to change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.680
and hair grows in new places. This&nbsp;
continues into their mid-to-late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:20.320
teens. Puberty also brings the start of&nbsp;
their menstrual periods. Technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:26.320
they can now become pregnant,&nbsp;
give birth, and feed their baby.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.400
They also have elevated levels of the&nbsp;
hormone estrogen, are highly social,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.880
and tend to show more empathy. Physically&nbsp;
not as strong as their male counterparts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:45.880
they exhibit a greater capacity to endure pain&nbsp;
and can rival men in ultra-endurance runs.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.560
Boys enter puberty a bit later, with&nbsp;
a growth spurt that peaks around age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.640
14. They develop more muscle mass,&nbsp;
body hair, and a larger Adam’s apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.880
which gives them a deeper voice that&nbsp;
can intimidate some and attract others.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.880
Boys also produce more testosterone and develop a&nbsp;
higher aggression potential. They are now larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:17.920
faster, significantly stronger, and&nbsp;
about 10x more likely to end up in jail.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.360
Some teens now identify as a gender different&nbsp;
from the sex assigned to them at birth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.680
about 3-10% enter their reproductive&nbsp;
years with an interest in the same sex.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.160
During their Reproductive Years,&nbsp;
and until their mid-thirties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:43.560
both sexes are typically very good at making&nbsp;
babies. But they face two very different risks.
 &nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:50.080
Men can technically make a baby in minutes.&nbsp;
Women however have to invest many months into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:57.200
pregnancy and give birth — with vast physical&nbsp;
and psychological consequences. This puts them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:05.000
at a lot of risk if the relationship breaks&nbsp;
apart. However, they do have one advantage.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.520
Once the newborn arrives, mothers can be certain&nbsp;
that the infant is theirs. A father that loves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.680
and cares for a child risks finding out it&nbsp;
is not his. And depending on the culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:27.640
1-10% do unknowingly raise another man's&nbsp;
child. So what’s mama’s baby, is papa’s maybe.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:33.080
A woman's anxiety about becoming a mother&nbsp;
with no help supporting the family and a man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:40.560
fear of paternal fraud is said to create&nbsp;
very different concerns about cheating.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:47.000
One theory hence suggests that men tend to worry&nbsp;
more about sexual infidelity. They want to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.960
sure they're raising the child they fathered.&nbsp;
Women typically get more jealous when they feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:59.560
emotionally betrayed. They worry that precious&nbsp;
family resources are shared with someone else.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:08.800
In Mid Adulthood, men and women begin to&nbsp;
lose some of their ability to make babies.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.000
If we study fertility by Age we see that&nbsp;
women’s fertility drops from their early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.760
thirties and by their forties, chances of getting&nbsp;
pregnant naturally are slim. Around their fifties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:28.640
they enter menopause and their periods stop. Men&nbsp;
remain reproductive much longer, but from age 35,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:37.400
the chance of producing a faulty sperm cell&nbsp;
increases exponentially with each passing year.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:44.600
As they reach Late Adulthood, women live longer.&nbsp;
Men die about 6 years younger, often of heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:52.520
failure. Perhaps because they don’t care as much&nbsp;
about their health and live a more risky life.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:58.160
What do you think? How do sex differences&nbsp;
influence our society and how does society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.440
influence them? And should we&nbsp;
create laws and regulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:09.760
to purposefully reduce the differences?&nbsp;
